om  bilbo on his 33rd birthday in the movie  version of that long expected party mary  and pippen are practically full  full-grown when they get into trouble  with gandalf this gives the  understandable impression that very  little time passes between that event  and the moment at which the four hobbits  begin their quest the truth is though in  the book version of the fellowship of  the ring a whopping 17 years elapse  between frodo getting the ring and frodo  leaving the shire and that's 17 years  exactly in fact he receives the ring on  his 33rd birthday and departs the shire  on his 50th in the novel this goes a  long way in explaining why he's so  attached to the ring right from the  get-go  while john noble does a fantastic job  portraying denathore on the silver  screen the cinematic version of the  steward of gondor doesn't exactly do  justice to the book he's arrogant to a  fault blinded by pride and literally  tells his men to leave minas tirith when  it comes under attack  [Music]  worst of all he orders faramir to attack  oscar leath when there's no question  that he's going to be moaned down by an  army of orcish archers all of this  behavior is so over the top that it begs  the question what the heck is going on  well if you turn to the original text  the answer becomes clear namely the fact  that his poor judgment is far more  nuanced in the books denethor isn't a  mad tyrant openly trying to kill his son  on the contrary while he does make his  disappointment in vermeer clear he  doesn't send his son on a suicide  mission instead when faramir retreats to  ministerith to report on the situation  in ascalia denathor simply asks him to  return and bolster the defenses more  generally the steward's hopeless despair  comes about because he has been battling  sauron's will through one of the  palantir all in all the book version of  denathor is a deeply flawed man but he's  also strong and noble and gives  everything he has to the defense of  gondor  hobbi